## Why This Is Asked
This tests advanced bit manipulation skills, requiring multi-word arithmetic without 128-bit native support. It demonstrates branchless programming techniques and careful handling of shift operations.

## Key Concepts
- Represent 128-bit values as two 64-bit words (hi, lo)
- Decompose k into t = k mod 64 (intra-word shift) and b = k div 64 (cross-word flag)
- Branchless selection using bitwise operations and masks
- Handle edge cases like k=0 and k=64 without conditionals

## Code Example
```c
void rotate_left_128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, unsigned k, 
                    uint64_t* out_hi, uint64_t* out_lo) {
    uint64_t t = k & 63;
    uint64_t b = (k >> 6) & 1;

    uint64_t hiA = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loA = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t hiB = (lo << t) | (hi >> (64 - t));
    uint64_t loB = (hi << t) | (lo >> (64 - t));

    *out_hi = hiA ^ ((hiA ^ hiB) & -b);
    *out_lo = loA ^ ((loA ^ loB) & -b);
}
```

## Examples
- k=1, hi=0, lo=1 → out_hi=1, out_lo=0
- k=64, hi=1, lo=0 → out_hi=0, out_lo=1

## Why This Is Asked
Rotations are fundamental in cryptography (e.g., SHA-2), hashing, and bitboard manipulation. Implementing them correctly on composite 128-bit types tests understanding of cross-word boundaries and careful handling of edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Decompose 128-bit value into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Handle k near 64 where rotation crosses half boundaries
- No loops required—just careful bitwise arithmetic
- Return early for k==0 to avoid undefined behavior with 64-bit shifts

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotl128(uint128 x, unsigned k) {
    k &= 127;
    if (k == 0) return x;

    if (k < 64) {
        uint64_t hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
        uint64_t lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    } else {
        unsigned k2 = k - 64;
        uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k2) | (x.hi >> (64 - k2));
        uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k2) | (x.lo >> (64 - k2));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend classic bit-trick patterns to wider word sizes without branches, a common need in performance-critical systems.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register)
- 128-bit data layout with two 64-bit halves
- Branchless bit counting and aggregation

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

static inline uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;
}

int popcount128(uint128 a) {
  return (int)(popcount64(a.hi) + popcount64(a.lo));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for SIMD-enabled languages (e.g., intrinsics)?
- Compare branchless approach vs. lookup-based methods on large datasets.

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests depth in bit twiddling and constant-time operations; parity computation is fundamental in checksums, error detection, cryptography, and performance-critical code paths.

## Key Concepts
- Parity reduction across word boundaries by folding upper and lower halves
- Fixed-time parallel reduction using shifts and XOR
- No branches; constant-time execution regardless of input

## Code Example
```c
unsigned int parity128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t p = x.hi ^ x.lo;
  p ^= p >> 32;
  p ^= p >> 16;
  p ^= p >> 8;
  p ^= p >> 4;
  p ^= p >> 2;
  p ^= p >> 1;
  return p & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to arbitrary bit widths or for streaming parity computation?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about cross-boundary bit interactions and implement constant-time checks using only bitwise ops. 

## Key Concepts
- Bitwise shifts and masks across multiword values
- Detecting adjacent bits within each 64-bit half and across the 64-bit boundary
- Branchless boolean results for interview clarity

## Code Example
```javascript
int hasAdjacentOnes128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.lo & (x.lo << 1);
  uint64_t b = x.hi & (x.hi << 1);
  int cross = ((x.lo >> 63) & 1) & (x.hi & 1);
  return (a | b | cross) != 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to detect a run of exactly k adjacent ones? 
- How would you implement a similar check for 256-bit values represented as two uint128s?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests precise understanding of bit-rotation, modulo-64 behavior, and edge cases without branching.

## Key Concepts
- Rotation via (x << s) | (x >> (64 - s)) with s = n & 63
- Safe when s = 0 using masking (64 - s) & 63
- No loops or conditionals; only shifts and ORs

## Code Example
```javascript
// JavaScript BigInt version for 64-bit rotation
function rotl64(x, n) {
  const s = BigInt(n & 63);
  const MASK = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFn;
  return ((x << s) | (x >> ((64n - s) & 63n))) & MASK;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for 128-bit types?
- What compiler optimizations help ensure constants are folded efficiently?

